// SWARM RABBIT HOLE — Wave 15 (Impl-T, Cohort Delta): Salt ε — the
// TYPED degradation of a Tier N atom in a PLAIN (non-verified) fn
// (A4 §4's rule 2, the mirror of E0701's rule 1). The atom
// 'x * y >= 0' is stamped Tier N (nonlinear product) at extraction;
// in a proof fn it degrades to a typed runtime assertion carrying
// the classification — the W-CONTRACT warning names the tier, and
// the runtime message names it too.
//
// NOTE the operand shape: a site whose operands ALL fold to constants
// (area3(2, 3)) discharges by plain constant evaluation — sound for
// every tier; the degrade is the site whose product is a RUNTIME
// value (the stamp's "never dischargeable" claim is about the
// symbolic atom — the DBM engine cannot prove a product, and this
// site's x is opaque). The valid input runs clean; the result is
// discarded so the golden output stays deterministic.

fn area3(int x, int y) -> int requires(x * y >= 0) {
    return x * y
}

fn main() {
    // a runtime value in [1, 100]: positive, so the product holds and
    // the typed runtime assertion passes silently
    int xv = time_now_ms() % 100 + 1
    area3(xv, 3)
    print("ok")
}
